# AFL ladder derivation — 1998–2023

Derived the `drv_*` columns that drive the AFL ladder for the historical seasons
(the import wrote raw player_match facts only). Script: `derive-ladders.sql`.

## What was set

1. `club_match.drv_score` = Σ(player goals)×6 + Σ(player behinds) + rushed_behinds
   — 10,096 club-innings (1998–2023 final matches). The rushed-behinds backfill
   was the prerequisite; without it scores would undercount.
2. `club_season` ladder standings (played/won/lost/drawn/for/against/premiership
   points) — 441 club-seasons, from home-and-away results only (finals excluded).

## Validation

- Run against 2026 (finals-excluded), the ladder SQL reproduced the
  domain-computed (`CalculateLadder`) `club_season` standings **exactly — 0
  mismatches**. So the SQL matches the tested Go ladder logic.
- Spot check — 2000: Essendon 21–1, 84 pts, 159.1% at the top (their famous
  minor-premiership season; 22 H&A games confirms finals were excluded).

## Notes / follow-ups

- `club_match.drv_premiership_points` deliberately left untouched — it's a
  vestigial column (premiership points live on `club_season`; nothing reads the
  club_match one). Not needed for the ladder.
- 2024 and 2025 are empty fixture scaffolds in the seed (all `no_data`, no player
  stats), so no ladder is derivable for them — only 1998–2023 (imported) and 2026
  (live) have data.
- **Finals-exclusion gap in the app:** `domain.CalculateLadder` /
  `FindFinalBySeasonID` filter on `data_status='final'` (completeness), not round
  type, so they'd include finals in the ladder. Only safe today because 2026 has
  no finals yet. When the derive step is built into the import, it must exclude
  finals rounds (this SQL does).
